<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashur-nadin-shumi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 694 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashur-nadin-shumi was the eldest son of the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a>, who installed him as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 700 BCE. In 694 BCE, while Sennacherib campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> handed him over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a>, who carried him off; he was never heard of again and was probably killed. His loss provoked Sennacherib's campaigns against Babylon and Elam, ending in the destruction of Babylon in 689 BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aššur-nādin-šumi, whose Akkadian name means &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashur-god">Ashur</a> gives a name&quot;, was probably the firstborn son of the Neo-Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> and his first crown prince, and so the designated successor to the Assyrian throne. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> had been conquered by Tiglath-Pileser III (r. 745–727 BCE) less than thirty years before he became its king. In those years Babylonia repeatedly tried to become an independent kingdom again, and Babylonian revolts were an especially frequent nuisance during Sennacherib's reign, which required him to defeat numerous southern uprisings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After defeating uprisings in 700 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> named his own son king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. Aššur-nādin-šumi was also titled māru rēštû, which can mean either the pre-eminent son or the firstborn son. The appointment and the title suggest that he was being groomed to follow Sennacherib as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, as Assyrians in most cases followed the principle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a>. Further evidence is that Sennacherib built a palace for him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>, as he would later do for the crown prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a>.</p><p>His position as Assyrian king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> was politically important and highly delicate, and would have given valuable experience to the intended heir of the whole empire. His tenure did not last long, however, and he was unable to handle the volatile political situation in the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 694 BCE Sennacherib campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, in what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, to pursue Chaldean rebels who had fled there. In response to this incursion the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> invaded the southern parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a>. Probably encouraged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> themselves, they captured Aššur-nādin-šumi at the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a>. The prince was taken back to Elam and probably executed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aššur-nādin-šumi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Provocations, including the handover and death of Sennacherib's son <b>Ashur-nadin-shumi</b> in Elamite captivity in 694 BCE, brought devastating reprisals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.9,52.4&amp;year=-704" aria-label="Elam on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5912 1072 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ashur-nadin-shumi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6362 1342h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6362 1342h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=29.9,52.4&amp;year=-704">What was here in 704 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
